+# Agent Note: Permission and approval failures reach the UI
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-17-permission-and-approval-failures-reach-ui.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The composer optimistically disabled permission controls and approval buttons, but dropped rejected command or response receipts. A failed permission command cleared the optimistic selection without telling the operator; a failed approval answer re-enabled the buttons without explaining whether the Host rejected the decision or the transport failed. Remote users could not distinguish an unchanged authoritative permission from a successful change.
+
+## Decision
+
+Permission submission clears its transient optimistic pick on every settlement. A rejected or thrown command opens a retryable modal containing the original error, keeps the Host-projected current value as the trigger label, and retries the same permission id. Full access retry reuses only the already confirmed choice; it does not bypass the risk confirmation for a different id.
+
+Approval answer failures re-arm the single-flight action row and render the original error in an alert. The pending carrier remains authoritative and the panel remains mounted until its resolved frame arrives. Task locking and carrier replacement continue to clear stale local state through the existing owner lifecycle.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Leave the controls disabled after failure.** Rejected because a transient relay or Host response failure would require abandoning the current Session to recover.
+
+**Treat a rejected command as a successful local permission change.** Rejected because the client projection is Host-owned; displaying the optimistic id after rejection would misrepresent the permission actually applied to the next tool call.
+
+**Use a generic toast with no retry action.** Rejected because permission and approval operations are consequential and the initiating controls remain the only unambiguous retry target.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The composer has two additional localized error surfaces and retains no client-only permission authority. Error messages may include Host-provided platform detail and are kept in the existing browser process; no new wire or durable fields are introduced. Approval response retries remain explicit and single-flight.
+
+## Testing
+
+The InputBar suite covers permission rejection, authoritative-value rollback, same-id retry, and a resolved retry. The ApprovalFlow suite covers a rejected response, re-arming, retry, and duplicate-click suppression. Focused GUI tests pass; the clean isolated typecheck remains blocked by pre-existing generated remote artifacts unavailable in this worktree.
